1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,976
The AIS feed publishes dozens of fixes per
second. The operator UI on a congested
laptop paints one map update at a time.

2
00:00:09,096 --> 00:00:23,399
Without a demand signal, the reactive
chain buffers until memory hurts, latency
spikes, then the console collapses.
Schedulers placed work on threads.
Backpressure answers how much work is
allowed to be outstanding.

3
00:00:23,519 --> 00:00:35,759
In Reactive Streams, the subscriber tells
the publisher how many items it requests
through the Subscription. Publishers
should respect that demand. That
conversation is backpressure.

4
00:00:35,879 --> 00:00:46,007
Classic imperative code hides the same
problem behind thread pools and blocking
queues; reactive makes the demand signal
explicit. Look at this java for a moment.

5
00:00:46,127 --> 00:00:52,631
Those two chains are different policies
for the same producer/consumer mismatch —
not interchangeable knobs.

6
00:00:52,751 --> 00:01:02,135
Buffering with onBackpressureBuffer(n)
keeps up to *n* fixes when the consumer
hiccups, then applies an overflow policy
(error, drop oldest, and so on).

7
00:01:02,255 --> 00:01:13,127
Use it for short bursts you cannot afford
to lose — a brief UI freeze while
archiving still wants those positions.
Always bound the buffer; unbounded buffer
is a deferred OOM.

8
00:01:13,247 --> 00:01:26,951
Dropping with onBackpressureDrop()
discards new signals when demand is zero.
Use it when a missed AIS tick is
acceptable and crashing is not — some
telemetry fits here. It is wrong for
ledger-like persistence.

9
00:01:27,071 --> 00:01:39,599
Latest-value with onBackpressureLatest()
keeps only the newest unread fix; older
pending values lose. That fits operator
map gauges, where a stale ship position is
worse than a skipped intermediate one.

10
00:01:39,719 --> 00:01:51,071
It is wrong when every event must be
processed. Rate shaping — limitRate, or
flatMap concurrency — controls how demand
is requested upstream or how many inner
publishers run at once.

11
00:01:51,191 --> 00:02:04,727
That is still backpressure: you are
limiting outstanding work, even when the
method name does not start with
onBackpressure. Prefetch is part of the
same story. Many operators request a batch
ahead for throughput.

12
00:02:04,847 --> 00:02:17,207
That helps until the batch is larger than
the consumer can absorb — then you are
back to choosing buffer, drop, latest, or
fail. There is no universal default that
saves you from naming the product policy.

13
00:02:17,327 --> 00:02:30,935
In WebFlux, the HTTP response and Netty
watermarks participate in demand. If you
return a Flux as SSE for
/positions/stream, a slow client should
slow generation when the pipeline is wired
correctly.

14
00:02:31,055 --> 00:02:41,471
If you assemble an in-memory list with
collectList() first, you already opted out
of streaming backpressure for that payload
— fine for small pages, dangerous for
unbounded AIS history.

15
00:02:41,591 --> 00:02:56,639
A misconception is “reactive automatically
prevents OOM.” It prevents OOM only when
operators and sources honor bounded
demand. A blocking JDBC Flux created by
hammering a cursor without limits can
still blow memory.

16
00:02:56,759 --> 00:03:11,087
Another misconception is using unbounded
onBackpressureBuffer() and calling it
production-ready. Name the bound. A third
is confusing backpressure with circuit
breaking — related resilience themes,
different mechanisms.

17
00:03:11,207 --> 00:03:15,527
We now have publishers, thread control,
and demand control.

18
00:03:15,647 --> 00:03:27,000
The missing piece in the Spring stack is
the web layer that speaks HTTP with these
types end to end — routers, annotated
controllers returning Mono/Flux, and a
runtime that is not DispatcherServlet.

19
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:29,903
That layer is Spring WebFlux.
